<section>
  <h2>How the runs are made (input-driven version)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Start:</b> today’s GFS analysis winds at 850, 700, 500 and 200 hPa (observed state, no forecast steps), with the seedling, any Arabian Sea low and the south-west Bay circulation taken out; those two are put back as swirls that move along each member’s chosen path.</li>
    <li><b>Future winds = today’s winds changed by the inputs, step by step in time.</b> 22 inputs, each with a low / as-observed / high setting (full list in the table above): when and where the depression forms, the ridge (position now north–south and east–west, drift, strength trend, and whether it breaks over the central Bay with south-westerlies from day 2–3), the Myanmar–Thailand high, the next westerly trough (timing, depth, how far south it digs; it moves east by itself), the MJO (fades and moves east / holds / strengthens), cross-equatorial westerlies, north-east monsoon easterlies, an easterly wave from the South China Sea, the 200 hPa jet, the Arabian Sea low and south-west Bay circulation (each along its own path, if present), sea temperature, shear and storm size. No forecast model and no past-storm winds are used.</li>
    <li><b>51 combinations, no random numbers:</b> member 0 has every input as observed; members 1–50 come from a fixed balanced design: each setting of each input is used in 16–17 members, and for every pair of inputs all 9 combinations of their settings appear at least 3 times.</li>
    <li><b>Track and strength:</b> our steering model (fitted on every Bay storm since 1980) moves the storm with these winds; strength from our fitted 12-hour change model, with inland decay over land.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
<section>
  <h2>How far to trust it</h2>
  <ul class="note">
    <li>This version cannot be tested on past storms the way the others were, because its inputs describe how the winds will change, and those changes are only known afterwards.</li>
    <li>Today the storm sits well south of the ridge (about 19°N) in strong easterlies with a slight northerly push, so on its own it moves west-south-west. What turns it north in these runs is mainly the ridge break (south-westerlies over the central Bay from day 2–3), then the ridge drifting north and a deep trough. The ‘Which inputs move the landfall’ chart shows this for each run.</li>
    <li>Each setting is used by about a third of the members, so the shares show how the outcome splits across all these possibilities, not how likely each possibility is. ECMWF and GFS currently expect the ridge to break.</li>
    <li>Strength: treat as one category either way. Shares are of these 51 members, not calibrated probabilities.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
</div>
